walkrie (1.2.1~alpha1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Added RPM packaging (packaging/rpm/), mirroring the existing .deb
    layout: same walkrie system user/group, /etc/walkrie,
    /var/lib/walkrie/{models,backfill} paths, and systemd unit. See
    packaging/rpm/walkrie.spec and TECHNICAL.md's "Installation (RPM
    package)" section.
  * LlamaProvider gained optional GPU offload via a new n_gpu_layers
    config field (CUDA, requires -DGGML_CUDA=ON at build time); silently
    falls back to CPU-only with a warning if the running build has no
    GPU backend. See PERFORMANCE.md Section 5 for measured effect
    (hardware-dependent, not a fixed win) and TECHNICAL.md's Known
    Limitations.
  * CMakeLists.txt's SQLite3 detection now checks the system version and
    falls back to fetching/building the amalgamation from source when it
    predates 3.35.0 (RETURNING clause support) instead of a hard
    find_package(SQLite3 REQUIRED) that would fail outright on older
    systems (e.g. RHEL/Rocky 9's stock sqlite-devel).
  * test_sink_batch_mode's single-vs-batch embedding comparison now
    gates on cosine distance instead of raw per-dimension max_abs_diff,
    since the latter isn't equivalently strict across providers with
    different output vector scales; max_abs_diff is still printed as
    diagnostic context on a mismatch.

 -- bugra <bugraaktug@gmail.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0300

walkrie (1.2.0~alpha1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * LSN acknowledgment is now tied to durable sink writes, not to WAL
    parsing. Previously the confirmed LSN advanced the instant a message
    was parsed, so a crash between decode and a sink's durable write
    caused silent data loss on restart. EventDispatcher now tracks each
    source's confirmed LSN per batch and only advances it once every
    required sink has durably applied that batch. See TECHNICAL.md's
    Isolation Layer section.
  * New per-sink durability tiers: each sink is `required` (default;
    failure blocks confirmation and retries) or best-effort
    (`required = false` in that sink's `[[sink]]` block; failure is
    logged and skipped). A required sink retries with exponential
    backoff (500ms initial, 60s stall ceiling) before triggering a
    graceful shutdown rather than looping forever or silently dropping
    data.
  * Fixed a related LSN-correctness bug: every ChangeEvent was stamped
    with the WAL position of its own row-change message rather than its
    transaction's real commit LSN, and Commit messages produced no event
    at all — so the confirmed LSN could never reach a transaction's true
    commit boundary, risking replay of already-applied transactions on
    restart. Commit messages now yield an Op::Commit marker event
    carrying the correct commit LSN. See TECHNICAL.md's Decode Layer
    section.
  * Initial backfill scan (WLK-0001): `backfill = true` on a `[[source]]`
    dumps pre-existing rows (as of that source's replication slot
    creation) into a local per-source SQLite staging store, then drains
    them via a separate `walkrie_worker` process running concurrently
    with live CDC streaming. Live events for still-pending rows are
    reconciled into the staged copy rather than raced against it, so the
    two paths never conflict, and crash-resume covers both an
    interrupted dump and a worker that died mid-drain. See TECHNICAL.md
    section 5 and PERFORMANCE.md section 7 for a real end-to-end
    (OpenAI) throughput benchmark.
  * Fixed a silent data gap: TRUNCATE (including CASCADE) on a watched
    source table previously produced no ChangeEvent at all, leaving the
    corresponding sink rows behind indefinitely. pgoutput_parser now
    decodes Truncate messages, emitting one ChangeEvent per relation
    named in the message (a single CASCADE truncate can name several).
    PgEmbeddingSink applies each as a bulk DELETE against the sink table,
    scoped to that table mapping's discriminator when one is configured;
    without a discriminator, the delete has no WHERE clause and removes
    every row in the (shared) sink table — logged as a warning. See
    TECHNICAL.md's Decode Layer / Sink Layer sections and the "Note on
    TRUNCATE" under discriminator config for details.
  * Fixed missing libsqlite3-dev in debian/control's Build-Depends and in
    the Dockerfile's build-stage apt-get list (present in CMakeLists.txt's
    find_package(SQLite3) requirement, needed by the new backfill staging
    store, absent from both since it was introduced).
  * Fixed a packaging gap in both the .deb and the Docker image: the
    `walkrie_worker` binary (spawned by `walkrie` to drain backfill work,
    resolved from the same directory as the `walkrie` binary itself) was
    never installed — CMakeLists.txt's install() rule only listed the
    `walkrie` target, and the Dockerfile only built/copied that same
    target. Any `backfill = true` source would have failed to spawn its
    worker on either install path. Now installed alongside `walkrie` in
    `/usr/bin/` in both.
  * Fixed a second backfill packaging gap, also in both the .deb and the
    Docker image: the default backfill SQLite staging directory
    (`/var/lib/walkrie/backfill`) was never created — `BackfillStore::
    open()` doesn't create its own parent directory, and only `models/`
    was staged (via debian/walkrie.dirs for the .deb, via the Dockerfile's
    `mkdir -p` for the image). Any `backfill = true` source would have
    failed to open its store on either install path. `backfill/` is now
    created (and chowned, via the existing recursive `/var/lib/walkrie`
    postinst step / Dockerfile chown) alongside
    `models/`.
  * THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md now documents SQLite (Public Domain, system
    dependency via libsqlite3-dev) — missing since the backfill staging
    store introduced it as a dependency. Also now actually shipped in the
    .deb (usr/share/doc/walkrie/) — debian/walkrie.install never listed
    it alongside README/TECHNICAL/PERFORMANCE.
  * Added a GitHub Actions CI workflow: build + unit test (ctest) on
    every push/PR to main.
  * Known limitation: per-sink queues (isolating one stuck required sink
    from the rest of the pipeline) remain a stated future direction, not
    yet implemented — a stuck required sink still stalls the whole
    pipeline.

 -- bugra <bugraaktug@gmail.com>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0300

walkrie (1.1.0~alpha1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Docker packaging: multi-stage Dockerfile (statically-linked llama.cpp,
    non-root runtime image), docker-compose.sample.yml, README/TECHNICAL.md
    Docker install docs. No config or model file baked into the image —
    same reasoning as the .deb package's config validation.
  * Optional event batching (batch_size/batch_timeout_ms in [app]) plus a
    real OpenAIProvider::embed_batch() implementation (one HTTP request
    for N texts instead of N requests) — measured ~7.3x lower per-row
    latency at batch_size=10. See PERFORMANCE.md Section 5.
  * LlamaProvider now implements real multi-sequence batched embedding
    (embed_batch() via n_seq_max), not the default per-embed() loop.
    Measured ~20% slower per row than sequential calls on tested hardware,
    unlike OpenAI's batching (~7.3x faster) — see PERFORMANCE.md and
    TECHNICAL.md's Known Limitations for the numbers and working hypothesis.
  * Correctness finding: embed()/embed_batch() match within epsilon for
    Q8_0 quantization but diverge meaningfully for Q4_K_M — see
    TECHNICAL.md's Known Limitations.
  * Fixed missing libspdlog-dev in debian/control's Build-Depends (present
    in CMakeLists.txt's find_package(spdlog) requirement but absent from
    the package's declared build dependencies since alpha1).
  * Known limitation: local Llama batching is implemented but not yet a
    throughput win — see PERFORMANCE.md and TECHNICAL.md.

 -- bugra <bugraaktug@gmail.com>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0300

walkrie (1.0.1~alpha1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Initial alpha release.
  * Real-time WAL streaming via native logical replication.
  * Local (llama.cpp) and OpenAI embedding providers.
  * postgres-embedding and json-output sink types.
  * Static discriminator support for multi-source sink tables.
  * Known limitation: single-threaded, unbatched embedding calls —
    See PERFORMANCE.md. Batching is planned for
    a future release.

 -- bugra <bugraaktug@gmail.com>  Mon, 20 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0300
